The C code picks its behavior at compile time with `#ifdef ENABLE_LOCALES`. The Rust equivalent is a `cfg` feature check. Since only `libc` is allowed as a dependency, I'll use `cfg!(feature = "enable_locales")` to choose between the two branches.

`cfg!` evaluates the condition but still compiles both branches, so both must be valid. `libc::localeconv` exists on Unix targets, so that branch compiles there. A runtime `if` on `cfg!` is the simplest route that keeps one function. Using `#[cfg]` on statements inside the function is also allowed, so I'll use that to avoid compiling the `libc` call when the feature is off.

The function is `static` in C, so it is private in Rust (no `pub`).

----FUNCTION----
```rust
fn get_decimal_point() -> u8 {
    #[cfg(feature = "enable_locales")]
    {
        unsafe {
            let lconv = libc::localeconv();
            return (*(*lconv).decimal_point) as u8;
        }
    }
    #[cfg(not(feature = "enable_locales"))]
    {
        return b'.';
    }
}
